WEBVTT

00:00:00.621 --> 00:00:01.951
Hi!

00:00:01.951 --> 00:00:03.397
In the next few minutes,

00:00:03.397 --> 00:00:07.225
I'll show you how SmartCodeGenerator turns a spreadsheet into a complete,

00:00:07.225 --> 00:00:09.360
working application.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:16.279
Screens, validation rules, sample data and tests, all from one command: build app.

00:00:17.075 --> 00:00:19.460
It starts in the console.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.653
I type build app, describe what I want, an order management system,

00:00:23.653 --> 00:00:26.908
and point it at a CSV file of orders.

00:00:26.908 --> 00:00:31.033
SmartCodeGenerator opens a guided workflow in the browser.

00:00:42.062 --> 00:00:44.121
Here's the workflow.

00:00:44.121 --> 00:00:49.108
Down the left are ten steps, from describing the application to running it.

00:00:49.108 --> 00:00:52.312
The two I answered on the command line are already ticked,

00:00:52.312 --> 00:00:56.240
and I can click any finished step to go back and change it.

00:00:57.022 --> 00:01:01.621
One small touch before we start: the page has a theme picker.

00:01:01.621 --> 00:01:06.842
Dark, high contrast, or warm, whatever is easiest on your eyes.

00:01:06.842 --> 00:01:08.800
I'll stay on light.

00:01:09.622 --> 00:01:12.580
First, the kind of application.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:19.286
It can build desktop apps in WPF or WinForms, a Web API, a console app, a class library,

00:01:19.286 --> 00:01:21.473
or a background worker.

00:01:21.473 --> 00:01:26.940
I'll choose a Blazor web application, so we can run the result right here in the browser.

00:01:27.662 --> 00:01:30.737
The data step shows the file I gave it.

00:01:30.737 --> 00:01:37.482
It also reads JSON, text files, Excel workbooks, and tables straight from SQL Server.

00:01:37.482 --> 00:01:39.513
Or I can skip data altogether,

00:01:39.513 --> 00:01:44.000
and let a local AI model work out the classes from the description.

00:01:45.022 --> 00:01:47.824
Next, the look and features.

00:01:47.824 --> 00:01:52.199
I'll give it a company name, a dark theme, and an accent color.

00:01:52.199 --> 00:01:56.821
Validation, sample data and a login screen are already switched on,

00:01:56.821 --> 00:02:00.010
and the window title follows the company name.

00:02:00.822 --> 00:02:05.173
Now it reads all twenty four rows and checks the shape of the data.

00:02:05.173 --> 00:02:09.900
It noticed that Item one, two and three are a repeating group.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.546
Ticking the finding splits them into an Order Item class of their own,

00:02:13.546 --> 00:02:16.240
just as a database designer would.

00:02:17.022 --> 00:02:22.662
This is the model it built: orders and their items, with every field typed from the data.

00:02:22.662 --> 00:02:27.220
I can rename a class or a field, change a type, add or remove fields,

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.980
or ask why something was decided.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.950
I'll rename Customer to Customer Name.

00:02:35.102 --> 00:02:39.870
Better still, it proposes validation rules that hold in every row.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:42.253
Order numbers are unique.

00:02:42.253 --> 00:02:45.443
Status only ever holds four values.

00:02:45.443 --> 00:02:49.023
The order date never comes after the delivery date.

00:02:49.023 --> 00:02:54.128
And the total always equals the net amount, times one plus the tax rate.

00:02:54.128 --> 00:02:56.620
I'll accept the ones I want.

00:02:57.342 --> 00:03:02.928
For the status, I can set the order it moves in, draft, confirmed, shipped, delivered,

00:03:02.928 --> 00:03:05.469
and mark delivered as final.

00:03:05.469 --> 00:03:08.737
The generated app will refuse to move an order backwards,

00:03:08.737 --> 00:03:11.300
or change it once it's delivered.

00:03:12.182 --> 00:03:17.484
The output step shows the relationships, and how the classes become screens.

00:03:17.484 --> 00:03:21.820
It guessed that orders are master data, but an order is really a transaction,

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:23.682
so I'll change it.

00:03:23.682 --> 00:03:29.450
On the right is the whole run as a single command, ready to repeat or drop into a script.

00:03:30.262 --> 00:03:35.303
The entry template step picks how desktop entry forms lay out their fields.

00:03:35.303 --> 00:03:39.800
It doesn't apply to a Blazor app, and the page simply says so.

00:03:40.542 --> 00:03:42.562
Now I generate.

00:03:42.562 --> 00:03:47.341
It writes the project, a test project, and the imported rows as seed data,

00:03:47.341 --> 00:03:51.443
then compiles it, streaming the build log as it goes.

00:03:51.443 --> 00:03:53.540
I'll speed up the wait.

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:00.637
It compiled.

00:04:00.637 --> 00:04:03.776
One more click starts the application.

00:04:11.158 --> 00:04:15.431
And here it is, a working web app, with a sign in page.

00:04:15.431 --> 00:04:18.106
I'll sign in as the administrator.

00:04:18.918 --> 00:04:21.509
Every order from the spreadsheet is already here,

00:04:21.509 --> 00:04:24.780
complete with created and modified dates.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.256
Let me try to save a new order that's delivered before it was placed.

00:04:50.038 --> 00:04:55.886
And the rule I accepted refuses it, with a clear message saying exactly what's wrong.

00:04:56.598 --> 00:05:00.884
So I'll correct the delivery date, and add an item to the order.

00:05:00.884 --> 00:05:03.671
This time it saves, together with its line,

00:05:03.671 --> 00:05:06.886
and the new order is at the bottom of the list.

00:05:09.438 --> 00:05:13.826
That's the whole journey, from a CSV file to a validated, tested,

00:05:13.826 --> 00:05:16.758
running application in a few minutes.

00:05:16.758 --> 00:05:20.287
And if you close the browser halfway through, nothing is lost:

00:05:20.287 --> 00:05:24.688
the next build app offers to resume exactly where you left off.

00:05:24.688 --> 00:05:26.636
Thanks for watching!
